A||||7||southfla||rough idle at traffic lights when a/c is on||||5||
Z||000000||southfla||09-25-2000||07:39 PM||||When I stop at a traffic light with my a/c running my 87 fiero 4cyl idles rough, acting like it wants to stall. It doesn't stall very often, but I know something is wrong. It acts perfectly fine when the air is off. It has always done this. I have owned the car for a year and had the belt replaced twice. Both times the rough idle disappeared for about a week then came back. In August I had the belt replaced for the second time, got new plugs & wires, had the air intake system cleaned, had the TBI and idle air control valves cleaned, and had the injectors cleaned. Suddenly my car ran great and had excellent power and better gas milage but the rough idle with the air on at traffic lights persists. I throw it in neutral now because it is so annoying, and that makes it go away.<P>Some have suggested a new oxygen sensor. I was also told the motor mounts were worn when I bought the car. It is rather loud in the interior at highway speeds...but I don't know if motor mounts are possibly related to this. I don't want to pay $70 for an "engine diagnostic." Any ideas? Again this problem only happens when the air is on, otherwise my fiero is great.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-25-2000||08:20 PM||||Check the mounts. check the cradle bushings as well.<P>automatic tranny or stick? If it's an automatic read the article in my cave about the torque strut problem. (The extra load of the AC being on can agrivate the problem. The 4 cyl is always a little rough at idle the extra load can pull it down some more yet.)<P>Check the MAT sensor too. (In fact do this first now that I've read your post again.)It's in the intake to the right of the TBI. A damaged MAT will do some pretty strange things and not always set ECM codes. If the MAT is fried you want the Borg Warner or ACDelco ones that have plastic caged tips. (The MAT sensor can be affected by TBI cleaning agents. Carbon buildup on it will throw it off. It also doesn't like intake backfires.) look up more notes about MAT in the sensor quick ref in my cave.<P>Posibly the AC could need some work. If the compressor has a problem it could add extra load on the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-25-2000||08:27 PM||||P.S. Yes the O2 sensor could do this.<P>If they've never been done I'd replace the O2 TPS and MAT all at the same time.<P>O2 sensors are only rated for so many miles. <P>The TPS just plain wears out and usually gets flaky long before it dies. It's not realy well built and tends to collect dirt over time.<P>MAT is likely been cooked at least once.<P><BR>Check for vacuum leaks to. A small one could be enough to turd on it with the increased load of AC at idle.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||southfla||09-25-2000||09:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>P.S. Yes the O2 sensor could do this.<P>If they've never been done I'd replace the O2 TPS and MAT all at the same time.<P>O2 sensors are only rated for so many miles. <P>The TPS just plain wears out and usually gets flaky long before it dies. It's not realy well built and tends to collect dirt over time.<P>MAT is likely been cooked at least once.<P><BR>Check for vacuum leaks to. A small one could be enough to turd on it with the increased load of AC at idle.<P>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-25-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thanks for the insight!!<P>Just a couple questions since I'm actually a little naive about car tech stuff. What is the MAT sensor, I mean what does MAT stand for? Also what is TPS? MY torque strut( that dog bone looking thing) is old as hell. Is the entire engine compartment supposed to rock back an inch or so when you engage drive? Mine does. Thanks again.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000004||DjKid||09-25-2000||09:08 PM||||rock back an inch? my thoughts would be no.. anyone? mine does this, of course I have a very badly warn motor mount(25$fix) which is one reason my car is sitting parked in my backyard atm. <P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-26-2000||09:12 AM||||All the sensors are listed in my cave article "Sensor QRG." (The cave link is at the bottom of every forum page.)<P>Is this 87 4 cyl an auto or stick? If it's auto and you still have the stock "dog bone" an inch of movement is only the begining. Read the torque strut article for details.<P>Even with the strut problem delt with an inch engine movment isn't abnormal. There's that much play in the rubber motor mounts and cradle bushings. You should still check all the mounts to be sure none are spliting.<P>Now it the cradle itself is moving an inch, that is an issue. there isn't enough play in the front cradle mounts to allow that much unless they are totally shot. You can fix that "easy" enough with either polyurethane or AL cradle bushings. <P>I just did polly on mine. I think it's more than stiff enough for a street car but if it was going to be raced at all I'd have gone to aluminum. Even tho the polly is about as hard as rock it still does have some give to it. (I was really suprised how much tighter the car is with the new polly cradle set.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||southfla||12-17-2000||06:40 PM||||Well...its been awhile. Idle got worse...even when A/C was off. Got motor mounts (dog bone, etc) done on 12/1. Problem fixed when A/C is off...and 90% fixed when A/C is running. Well worth $250. Still a little rattle at 750 rpm...sometimes I hardly notice it. Once idle bottoms out at 600 at the stop lights everything is quiet.<P>Now I have a slow oil drip from the oil pan gasket, and that damn check engine light comes on once in awhile. Runs better than ever though. Does anyone recommend engine oil stop leak additive?||||reg||10
Z||000007||MrPBody||12-17-2000||08:44 PM||||No experience with engine oil stop leak additives, but I'm skeptical of their value.  The oil leak on my '85 4-cylinder came from the crankshaft (timing cover) seal -- something else to check.<BR>||||reg||1
